N2  - Summary ?Early-type galaxies? (ETGs) consist of the two historical classes of elliptical and lenticular galaxies. They play a key role in the formation of stars in the Local Universe. This chapter briefly discusses the properties of ETGs. ETGs span a very broad range of luminosity and mass profiles, ranging from galaxies with very low luminosity to extreme systems. ETGs with a large-scale disc satisfy the same trends as spiral galaxies; they also contain bars, rings and ?lenses?. Another important component of ETGs is their population of globular clusters, which seems to have a high specific frequency at both extreme ends of the mass spectrum, for galaxies with low and high luminosity. The chapter sheds light on the global properties of the stellar component in ETGs. It presents some of the global characteristics of the stellar populations of ETGs. The chapter discusses the dynamics and kinematics of ETGs based on observations and modeling.
